---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';

const snapshot = [
  { label: 'Organization', value: 'Local dental practice, single location, Northern Italy' },
  { label: 'Timeframe', value: 'Funnel data, January – April 2026' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Direct management of Google Ads and Meta Ads, tracking setup, funnel analysis, strategic recommendation' },
  { label: 'Channels', value: 'Google Ads Search (primary), Meta Ads (lead generation)' },
  { label: 'Service', value: 'Mostly implantology — high ticket, low volume' },
];

const results = [
  { metric: '37', label: 'patients acquired in four months' },
  { metric: '€200,013', label: 'revenue attributed to the campaigns' },
  { metric: '~€5,400', label: 'average value per patient' },
];
---
<CaseStudyLayout
  title="When advertising outpaces the company’s capacity"
  description="Case study: Google Ads and Meta for a high-ticket local practice. 37 patients in four months, right up to a fully booked schedule — and the decision to pull back."
  eyebrow="Google Ads · Meta Ads · Attribution and operational capacity"
  snapshot={snapshot}
  results={results}
>
  <h2>The context</h2>
  <p>
    A local dental practice, single location, specialized in implant treatments: high unit-value
    services with low volume. In a context like this, the game isn't played on big numbers — a few
    dozen patients a year is enough to substantially move revenue. Which makes measurement all the
    more important: every lost contact weighs far more than it would in a high-volume business.
  </p>

  <h2>The challenge</h2>
  <p>
    Generating qualified demand for an expensive treatment that the patient weighs for a long time
    before deciding — and, above all, actually understanding where patients come from. For a local
    practice, the phone rings for a thousand different reasons: word of mouth, walk-by signage,
    returning patients, organic search. Without a clean separation, any number on advertising
    effectiveness would have been a guess.
  </p>

  <h2>My role</h2>
  <p>
    Direct management of both channels: Search coverage on Google and lead generation campaigns on
    Meta. I set up tracking so that only contacts generated by the campaigns flowed into the
    collection sheets, and built, together with the practice, monitoring of the full journey —
    from first contact to first visit, through to accepted treatment and its value. The final
    recommendation to the client came out of discussion with the rest of the team.
  </p>

  <h2>Analysis and insight</h2>
  <p>
    Separating advertising contacts from everything else was the decision that made everything
    else possible. Only that way did the return figures stop being an estimate and become a
    defensible number.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second insight concerns the role of the two channels. Meta brought volume at a low cost —
    28 contacts at €25.96 each in a single month — but with a noticeably lower conversion rate to
    patient compared to the inquiries collected from Google search. Not one good channel and one
    bad one: two channels with different roles, where search intercepts people who've already
    decided to look into it and social builds demand on a colder audience that needs different
    timing and handling.
  </p>
  <p>
    The third insight is the one that changed the direction of the project: with high tickets and
    low volumes, the metric that really matters isn't cost per contact, but how many appointments
    the practice can physically handle.
  </p>

  <h2>Execution</h2>
  <p>
    Continuous Search coverage on Google to capture explicit demand, paired with a Meta lead
    generation campaign dedicated to implantology, with structured collection of contacts and
    follow-up preferences (channel and time slot) to make the front desk's job easier. Monthly
    funnel tracking shared with the practice: contacts, first visits, accepted treatments, value.
  </p>

  <h2>Results</h2>
  <p>
    In four months the campaigns produced 37 acquired patients, for €200,013 in attributed revenue
    against €2,142.48 in total ad spend across the two channels. The average value per patient is
    around €5,400: it's this — the high ticket of implantology — that explains an advertising
    return that, proportionally, exceeds 90 times spend. A number that only makes sense read
    together with the value of a single treatment, and that would be misleading to compare against
    benchmarks from low-ticket industries.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Meta campaign, taken on its own, generated 3 patients and €17,280 in revenue on €753 in
    spend: a solid return, with longer maturation times compared to search.
  </p>

  <h2>The outcome: pulling back</h2>
  <p>
    At a certain point the practice stopped being able to take new appointments: the schedule was
    full. Continuing to invest would have meant generating demand destined to go unmet — contacts
    that wait, cool off and end up elsewhere, with the side effect of wearing down the practice's
    reputation.
  </p>
  <p>
    So we paused the campaign and, together with the rest of the team, recommended the client go
    through an internal reorganization, to build the operational capacity needed to sustain that
    level of demand. It's a recommendation that, in the short term, reduces advertising activity:
    but suggesting the client spend on demand they can't serve would have hurt them and, in the
    long run, hurt us too.
  </p>

  <h2>What I learned</h2>
  <p>
    That marketing isn't always the bottleneck, and recognizing when it stops being one is part of
    the job. That with high tickets, contact quality matters more than contact cost — and
    different channels need to be judged on the role they play, not the same metric. And that
    without a clean separation of advertising contacts from everything else, any claim about
    advertising return remains an opinion.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>
